WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.780
Alien: Covenant hits theaters on May 19, and
fans couldn't be more psyched. Formerly known

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:10.760
as Prometheus 2 and Alien: Paradise Lost,
this latest installment in the Alien saga

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:16.450
continues the story set in motion by 2012's
divisive Prometheus. Here's why we think this

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:20.610
sci-fi horror flick is going to be a return
to form for the Alien franchise — and a

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.480
big step forward from the last movie.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.200
Xenomorphs

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.100
While it may or may not be a traditional Alien
movie, Alien: Covenant has at least one thing

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.020
that Prometheus didn't — xenomorphs.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.000
Yeah. Those guys.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.500
Like the poster for Alien: Covenant that was
revealed in March 2017, this trailer is crawling

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:47.391
with aliens. We've seen a facehugger spring
from an egg, a new backburster Neomorph, and

00:00:47.391 --> 00:00:52.739
multiple shots of xenomorphs in familiar confines
like spaceship hallways and creepy caves.

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:56.920
There's even a stare-down between a xenomorph
and Danny McBride, with nothing but a spaceship

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:03.340
windshield between them. What could that alien
want? Hopefully this:

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:07.880
H.R. Giger is all over it

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:12.960
Visual artist H. R. Giger is gone now, but
as long as the Alien franchise exists, his

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.130
ghost will haunt it. The guy was into some
spooky occult stuff. You don't make a book

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:21.700
like the Necronomicon and then peaceably surrender
to death's cold embrace. You stick around

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.090
and haunt stuff, starting with your very own
museum in Switzerland, and continue with a

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:27.640
whole bunch of Hollywood blockbusters.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:32.630
But back when Alien: Covenant was still Prometheus
2, director Ridley Scott seemed intent on

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.780
going in a new direction, stating publicly
that the film wouldn't feature any xenomorphs

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:37.280
at all:

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.900
"The beast is done. Cooked." Of course, we
know that's not the case anymore. It's unclear

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:46.320
what prompted the subsequent about-face, but
maybe Scott realized that Giger's influence

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.820
on Prometheus was actually its saving grace.
Say what you want about the directing, writing,

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.480
acting, and characters — that movie is at
least visually dazzling.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.270
Giger's Alien aesthetic — featuring things
like slime, biomechanics, a metallic black

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:03.829
and blue palette, and skeletal hinges — is
an intrinsic part of the franchise. And from

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:07.740
the looks of things, Scott and his team have
realized it. Alien: Covenant represents a

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.960
step in the right direction with regards to
honoring Giger's legacy and keeping his ghost

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:12.460
happy.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.480
Bringing back body horror

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.480
As important as Giger's concept art and designs
have always been, the xenomorph also owes

00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:24.779
just as much to screenwriter Dan O'Bannon's
original script. The alien's anatomical anomalies

00:02:24.779 --> 00:02:29.430
inspire real dread in the first Alien movie
— and that's by design. As O'Bannon said

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.769
in a 2002 retrospective, "[With Alien,] I'm
going to attack the audience."

00:02:33.769 --> 00:02:38.219
Like other great "body horror" movies, Alien
exploits moviegoers' fears of being physically

00:02:38.219 --> 00:02:42.959
violated by using grotesque imagery and gore.
Alien: Covenant's trailers and other promo

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:47.499
materials make prominent use of the series'
strongest quality — its clever use of "body

00:02:47.499 --> 00:02:48.499
horror" tropes.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:49.560
"My body is ready."

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:51.520
The new crew

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.799
Did you ever wonder why a group of scientists
would remove their helmets on an alien planet

00:02:55.799 --> 00:03:00.400
just because the air appeared to be breathable?
You're not alone. As Forbes' review put it,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.709
the characters in Prometheus "do ridiculously
dumb things. When they see black alien ooze,

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:08.749
they touch it. When they find a giant severed
alien head, they bring it on the ship and

00:03:08.749 --> 00:03:10.959
perform inexplicable experiments on it."

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.689
They're supposed to be scientists, yet they
behave like idiots, so it's hard to care when

00:03:14.689 --> 00:03:15.189
they die.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.440
That said, Alien's crew had its fair share
of bad decision-making, but it was always

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.730
grounded in the crew's mutual experience of
working for a big corporation that treated

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:28.260
them as expendable. The bond they shared with
each other was both professional and personal.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.359
The Nostromo's fate was horrific because audiences
actually cared about the crew.

00:03:32.359 --> 00:03:36.599
By all appearances, Alien: Covenant seems
to correct one of Prometheus's biggest missteps

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:41.310
— the aloofness of its crew of scientists
and specialists, which made them hard to sympathize

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:45.349
with. In the "Prologue: Last Supper" clip,
the new crew lets loose and throws a party

00:03:45.349 --> 00:03:50.140
to celebrate being the first colonists on
a mysterious planet. Recasting the main characters

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:54.420
as colonists aligns it more closely with Scott's
original film, and that's a good sign.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.060
Music to your ears

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.340
Composers Jerry Goldsmith and Lionel Newman's
incredibly tense score set Alien apart from

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:06.080
other horror movies. And if Alien: Covenant
seeks to recapture the feel of the first movie,

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.799
it'll need a comparable score. Fortunately,
there's a good bet that Jed Kurzel is up to

00:04:10.799 --> 00:04:14.670
the task. You might already know him as the
lead singer of the band the Mess Hall. He

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:19.060
knows how to create tension, and he's amassed
a serious résumé: he scored his brother

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:24.250
Justin Kurzel's 2011 psychological horror
film Snowtown. Plus, say what you will about

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:29.189
the rest of Assassin's Creed, but Kurzel's
epic score was undeniably solid. As the trailers

00:04:29.189 --> 00:04:33.629
for Alien: Covenant show, Kurzel lets the
onscreen action influence the music, allowing

00:04:33.629 --> 00:04:38.770
for low ambient tones in dark tense sequences,
stretches of silence during conversations,

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.980
and sweeping anthemic accompaniment for the
action-horror set pieces.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:43.720
Practical effects

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:47.960
Ridley Scott is known for having a healthy
distrust of CGI effects. What does that mean

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:52.580
for Alien: Covenant? More of the classic creature-feature
practical effects that made the first two

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:57.300
films in the series so frightening. To horror
fans who can't help but laugh at CGI monsters,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.600
this is welcome news.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.620
Ridley Scott knows what he's doing

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:05.340
We're confident that Ridley Scott will do
right by fans of Alien — and fans of Prometheus.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:10.139
There's a lot riding on Alien: Covenant. If
it hits the sweet spot in terms of box office

00:05:10.139 --> 00:05:13.930
grosses, Scott's promised as many as a half
dozen more Alien movies.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:17.990
As James Cameron put it, even "not-so-great"
Ridley Scott films are worth seeing "because

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:22.580
he is such an artist.” Put simply: Even
a mediocre Ridley Scott movie is still at

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:26.831
least intermittently compelling, and we don't
expect that to change anytime soon. Let's

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:31.140
keep our fingers crossed that Covenant is
the return to form we're all hoping for.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.940
Thanks for watching! Click the Looper icon
to subscribe to our YouTube channel. Plus

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:37.720
check out all this cool stuff we know you'll
love too!

